WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:05.030 align:start position:0%
 
at this moment  10 years ago the earthquake struck the  most powerful in japanese history  it was only the first blow in a triple  disaster that would take more than 18  000  lives today the emperor and the prime  minister  remembered so did all of japan  my heart aches when i turn my thoughts  to those who have struggled with various  hardships  with drastic changes of their living  conditions  including those who have lost close ones  such as family members or friends  and those who have lost their homes or  jobs and have been separated from the  people of their communities  a decade gone but only a moment ago for  those remembering the ones they lost  40 minutes after the earthquake the  tsunami thundering in  hit the shore it engulfed entire towns  for thousands there was no way out  i was forced to leave my hometown due to  the nuclear radiation  and it was listed as one of the  evacuation instruction zones  it was forbidden to enter before 2017  but even after the restriction was  lifted  the land around my house was so  radioactive that we had to tear it down  last september  now i've lost my home and the dwelling  has become a total wasteland  the third looming disaster soon became  clear  the fukushima nuclear plant had been  flooded too  over the next few days there were three  nuclear meltdowns it was the worst  accident since chernobyl  massive amounts of radiation were  released and more than 150  000 residents were evacuated  the plant remains out of service today  but this is an ongoing disaster  a cleanup operation that will take years  the spent fuel rods of the reactors are  still being removed and taken to storage  fukushima prompted a nationwide loss of  faith in nuclear power  one that endures to this day  after experiencing march 11 in tokyo and  knowing fukushima's situation  i came to the conclusion that nuclear  power plants are absolutely not needed  this is my strong belief and it hasn't  changed even after 10 years  that's why i came here it is a dilemma  for japan  which needs nuclear energy to meet its  renewable climate goals  the more immediate concern though is the  delayed 2020 tokyo olympics  they had been billed as the  reconstruction games foreign spectators  will not be allowed though a japanese  news agency has reported  the olympic torture relay will start in  two weeks  even in a pandemic and amid the enduring  pain of what happened  10 years ago it will also bring some  light  tom cheshire sky news beijing

